WEBVTT

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:03.560
Thank you.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:08.530
We have nice scents, madam.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:09.640
Would you like some?

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.580
Here you are.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.370
Madam, madam.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:16.970
We have nice scents, madam.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.410
Here you are.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.620
Oh, yes.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:34.420
Welcome aboard, Miss Debenham.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:35.140
Thank you.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:47.990
Here's your ticket, Monsieur Poirier.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.870
I'm afraid you've still got another hour.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.290
Please, do not wait.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:52.100
Not wait?

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.760
After all you've done for us, Monsieur Poirier.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.440
My general's orders were to ensure your safe departure.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.910
You also wish me to thank you again for saving the honor of the British garrison in Jordan.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.740
The brigadier's confession was opportune.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:08.870
I say.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:09.810
How did you do it?

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.490
Was it the old thumbscrew, you know, the rack, huh?

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:14.310
Yes.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.700
Well, you'll be able to rest as soon as you get to Stambool.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:24.660
The Church of Santa Sophia is absolutely magnificent.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.040
You have seen it?

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:27.630
No.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:55.880
I hope we did the right thing booking you into a hotel on the European side rather than the Asian side.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.240
I have no prejudice against either continent.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.150
The crossing should be pleasant. The Bosphorus is always calm.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.800
You have crossed by the ferry.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:12.770
No.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:30.880
A boss not.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:41.530
For us very shortly depart for Istanbul Sarcaj station, connecting with the Orient Express.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:53.380
Not now.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.960
When it's all over, when it's behind us, then.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:58.030
Achy!

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.130
What a funny little man.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:06.600
Obviously a frog.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:30.040
Thank you.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.280
Ecco finalmente un ami.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:47.210
Monsieur Guarón, good to see you.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.650
You have saved me from apoplexy, sit down.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:50.630
Ah, thank you.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:53.350
Dined well, eh?

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.640
Cures that are better quality than the kebab, the bottle is more distinguished.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.520
it's wild and the cafe fortunately i have been called to london and leave tonight

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:16.800
on the orient express how else if i have a traveling companion excellent well where shall we die

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.660
Yes, Monsieur Poirot, there is not one single first-class sleeping berth on the entire train.

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:37.360
What? In December?

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:38.980
In December, Signor Bianchi.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.360
Has Bulgaria declared war on Turkey? Are the aristocracy fleeing the country?

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:45.120
I am a director of the line.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.720
Monsieur Hercule Poirot is not only a detective of international fame and distinction,

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.380
but he is also my personal friend.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:51.940
Signor Bianchi, Monsieur Poirot.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:54.640
Ah, Courageo, my friend. We'll arrange something.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.280
And the army. We must go. Come on.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:11.660
Thank you very much.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:59.830
Orientex Part 1, Ozone, Korea, Nagreb, Chilean, Lausanne, Brussels, Paris, Calais.

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:01.790
Connections for London.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:06.380
Orient Express departs at 9 o'clock,

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:07.880
gate number 1,

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:12.380
to the destination of Sofia, Belgrade,

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.200
Zagreb, Rhone, Trieste,

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.810
Venice, Milan, Lausanne,

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.210
Bâle, Paris and Calais,

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:21.980
with the correspondence for London.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.260
Orient Express,

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:28.060
train, at 9 o'clock,

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.440
from Heron Pier.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:32.910
At the door, Sofia.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.540
Good evening, Pierre.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.300
Madame la Princesse, my respects.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:37.870
Number 14.

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:40.370
Howland Schmidt, welcome.

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:42.050
Number 6.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:43.580
Thank you.

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:44.040
You're welcome.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:56.600
Begriet!

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:57.860
Begriet!

00:13:58.140 --> 00:13:58.740
Begriet!

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:29.240
See you next time.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:30.620
And you are a cat.

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:19.170
Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! Bouz! I won!

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:28.780
Ah, Mrs. Hubbard, it's always an honour to greet you. You have your favourite number, 11.

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:40.240
Ecco, signor Bianchi. Benvenuto. Numero 9, come sempre.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.780
Grazie, Pierre. I understand that you are full up.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:44.960
It's unbelievable, signor. All the way to the top.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.440
world elects to travel to die.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.860
Nonetheless, you must find room for Monsieur Poirot

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:53.370
here. Monsieur Hercule Poirot?

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.260
The famous? Precisely.

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:57.940
And he is also a personal friend

00:15:57.940 --> 00:15:58.380
of mine.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.610
So good as to direct.

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:04.650
Oh, Frockenholzson, good afternoon.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.110
Place number 7.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:06.990
7, yeah.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:17.160
What thing is lost?

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.080
My little medal of St. Christopher

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:24.170
to bring me luck and deliver me from evil.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:29.720
Madame, Madame, lucky tooth from St. Augustine of HIPPO.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.020
Thank God, my Saint Christophef!

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.000
Saint Christophef!

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:09.800
You see, my friend has been urgently called to London on a matter of international importance

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:14.760
and I have given him my personal assurance that you will secure an accommodation for him on the coach to Calais.

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:17.070
But, Signor Garkey, I've already...

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:24.200
Excuse me, excuse me, gentlemen, but Mr. Ratchett has reservations and we'd be grateful if we could board immediately.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:28.740
Ah, Mr. Ratchett, welcome to the number 10.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:35.580
Mr. Beddows, the lower berth in number one, Mr. McQueen, the lower berth in number four.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.590
The upper berths are as arranged.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:47.990
Pierre, it is called. Now we can place Monsieur Parrault in the number 16, which is always kept vacant.

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:50.670
It is taken, senor, by Mr. Hartman.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:59.180
Then, as a director of the line, I command you to place Monsieur Parrault in what we know to be the empty berth above Mr. McQueen's number four.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:05.260
At least you can get two tips.

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.390
My second husband, Mr. Hubbard, would have raised hell.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:50.600
No place for my makeup bag, no ice in my drinking water,

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.880
and the hot water burps as it comes out of the faucet.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.320
Cowardage, my friend, it is the last compartment but one.

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:22.390
No, no, I think there's a mistake.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.630
Mr. McQueen, there is no other person on the train.

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:29.430
Mr. Poirot has to come in here.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.540
All is arranged. Yours is the upper berth to number four.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:35.020
We start in one minute.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:37.960
I apologize if I have incommoded you in.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:39.940
However, it is for one night only at Belgrade station.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:42.400
Oh, I see. At Belgrade? You're getting it?

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:00.660
Thank you.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.160
They'll probably keep the menu as a souvenir.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:29.880
Hector, I ordered three Islamic 13th century perforated pottery.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.580
three bowls and six beakers.

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:35.700
He delivered only five beakers, and one of the bowls arrived chipped,

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:39.140
which it was not when I paid for it, through the nose.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:41.320
Send a telegram from Belgrade.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:42.860
Yes. Yes, Mr. Ratchett.

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:45.290
What's the matter? You look tired.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:47.980
I slept badly.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:48.720
Yeah, why?

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:54.430
Belgian in the upper berth snored.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:58.020
Really? Any other unanswered letters on file?

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.580
Only the anonymous ones.

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.670
We can't answer those, can't we?

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.520
You better go catch up on your sleep

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.080
before the Belgian gentleman gets back to your compartment.

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:13.880
Go on.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.680
James Hardman, call me Dick.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:50.860
Poscarelli, call me Gino.

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:57.720
Beddows, Mr. Beddows.

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.570
Ah, for the pen of a Balzac.

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:06.310
For three days, all these people, these total strangers,

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:09.830
meet in a single train whose engine controls their destiny.

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:16.150
Yes, I know, we are both envious of the husband.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:21.660
Is the husband as British as his tweets?

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:24.200
Oh, heaven forbid, he's a hot-blooded Hungarian.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:27.200
If you but look at his wife, he will cease to be a diplomat.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:28.540
Thank God we are not young.

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:46.300
My second husband said always to ask for change in dollars or at worst, sterling.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.360
So for Pete's sake, what's a drachma?

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:52.160
It is what you call it, the currency.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:56.960
My second husband also said, take a book of food tickets, Mama, and you'll have no problem, no problem at all.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:58.380
That just isn't so.

00:24:58.800 --> 00:24:59.760
First, this is 10%.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.920
Five would have done the steward more than that.

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:02.560
I think Mrs. Alston has a headache.

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:05.280
Would you forgive us if we went back to the compartment, Mrs. Hubbard?

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:06.340
Oh, gladly, if you must.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.080
If you need aspirin, I always carry it on my person.

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:11.280
I mistrust foreign drugs.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.880
You must excuse me, Mrs. Hubbard is upon us.

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.470
What's the matter with him? Train sick or something?

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:22.250
Some of us, in the words of the Divine Greta Garbo, want to be alone.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:35.150
And for dinner this evening?

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:39.180
For goodness to serve me the poached soul

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:41.440
with one new potato

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:44.260
and a small green salad with new dressing.

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:48.410
Who is that majestic lady?

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:50.050
Princess Dragomiro.

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:51.630
I have heard.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:13.610
Pardon me, sir. I wonder if you could oblige me with a light.

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:14.510
Yes, certainly.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:18.000
Thank you. My name is Ratched.

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:21.750
Do I have the pleasure of speaking to Mr. Hercule Perot?

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.730
The pleasure, possibly, Mr. Ratched. The intention, certainly.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:28.450
You ask me for a light, I offered you one and you have not used it.

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:29.980
You can't deduce that.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.600
...without acute mental exhaustion.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:35.080
That's wonderful. Sit down, sir.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:35.920
For a moment.

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:38.020
Just for a moment. Thank you very much.

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:41.450
Well, Mr. Perot...

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:42.840
Eh, Poirot.

00:26:43.370 --> 00:26:43.750
How's that?

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:44.850
Poirot.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:46.580
Oh, Perot, right.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.120
I just wanted to say that in my country...

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:51.280
...we also come quickly to the point.

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:58.900
I want you to take a job on for me.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:03.350
It means big money, very big money.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:08.280
What is the case, as you put it, the job which you wish me to undertake?

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.600
Mr. Perot, I'm a rich man.

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.970
Naturally, men in my position have enemies.

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:16.890
Only one.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:19.900
What the hell do you mean by that?

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:23.820
Really that when a man is in a position to have, as you say, enemies,

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:28.100
it does not usually reserve itself into one enemy only.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:29.300
Oh.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.940
Sure, sure, I appreciate that.

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:33.350
What is your profession?

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:36.200
I'm retired.

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:37.830
From what?

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:38.820
Business.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.320
What sort of business?

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:43.480
Baby food.

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:45.200
What does that matter?

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:46.800
What matters is my safety.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:47.980
You are in danger.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.460
My life has been threatened, Mr. Perot.

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:54.080
My secretary can show you two letters on file.

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.760
I show you this.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:04.120
I sleep on it.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:11.230
Mr. Perot, $5,000.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.120
$10,000?

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:19.330
$15,000.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:25.450
Mr. Ratchet, I have made enough money to satisfy both my needs and my caprices.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:29.980
I take only such cases now as interests me, and to be frank, my interest...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.140
in your case is dwindling.

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:37.130
Ah, Monsieur Poirot, I'm transferring Signor Bianchi's luggage to the Pullman.

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:38.750
He's giving you his own compartment.

00:29:38.910 --> 00:29:40.790
But you cannot sit up all night!

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:43.390
Ah, my dear friend, do not concern yourself.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:47.940
Since you are going to England, it is better for you to stay on the through coach to Calais now.

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:49.540
Pierre has made me very comfortable.

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:52.560
There is no one in the Pullman but one Greek doctor.

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:55.660
Such generosity deserves my thanks!

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:29.840
Yes, Monsieur.

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:32.770
Who are my new neighbors?

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:36.320
To the left, Monsieur, the Swedish lady, Miss Alson,

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:39.160
who shared the seven and eight with the English lady, Miss Debenham.

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:42.660
And to the right, in number 10, is Mr. Ratchett.

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:44.560
And where is the loquacious Mrs. Hubbard?

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:45.980
I should like to get some sleep tonight.

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:48.380
Beyond Mr. Ratchett, in number 11.

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:49.480
She's still too near.

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:51.340
Good night, Mr. Beddow.

00:30:52.690 --> 00:30:53.130
Pardon.

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:01.680
Who is it?

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.140
It's me, sir, Bellows, with your seditude.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:16.120
Pierre, good night.

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:18.450
Good night, monsieur, and pleasant dreams.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:20.160
In number nine.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:23.320
How many drops?

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:26.330
Of the Valerian, two, sir, as you said.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:31.310
No, no, put it on the table.

00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:42.010
Tell Mr. McQueen I want to see the text of the telegram he sent from Belgrade.

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:47.150
Who's that?

00:31:57.850 --> 00:31:58.050
Monsieur.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:32.100
Come in.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:06.790
The beautiful countess.

00:34:00.508 --> 00:34:01.508
Thank you.

00:34:08.476 --> 00:34:08.976
Do they re-

00:34:09.052 --> 00:34:10.332
I'm in a nightmare!

00:34:12.668 --> 00:34:14.028
Ah, welcome to Ratchet!

00:34:14.652 --> 00:34:16.252
May you now have pleasant dreams.

00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:34.190
It's the silence of the world.

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:53.320
You're crazy!

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:55.020
You're crazy!

00:34:55.180 --> 00:34:55.440
You're crazy!

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:02.540
So now there's a man in my room.

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:04.260
I woke up at the dock three minutes ago

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:05.960
and there was a man hiding in this compartment.

00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:06.660
I sensed it.

00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:08.100
What's more, I know who he was

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:10.380
because I absent-mindedly nearly walked through his open door

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:11.240
earlier this evening.

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:12.780
Madam said this, Mr. Ratchett.

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:15.000
If you'd done this 20 years ago, I'd have said, come in.

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:16.120
20 years ago?

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:16.420
Ha!

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:17.760
But I'd only have been 15.

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:21.600
If there should be a reoccurrence,

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:23.370
do not hesitate to ring, madam.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:32.040
It's the end!

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:51.500
I have a new Christ!

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.100
It's me, sir, Beddows, with your pick-me-up.

00:36:27.910 --> 00:36:28.910
I'm the moon, Mr. Ratchet.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:48.710
Your passkey.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:55.540
Shame.

00:37:04.700 --> 00:37:05.760
I can't touch nothing.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:12.560
Senor Bianchi and the Greek doctor.

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:15.510
In the dining car, monsieur.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:16.820
Petch them at once.

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:39.200
Well, can't you tap the telephone wire?

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:40.600
Well, fire a rocket or something.

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:41.920
This is not a ship, madam.

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:42.400
Very exactly are we.

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:44.520
We are between Vinkovci and Broad.

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:45.780
But in what country?

00:37:45.940 --> 00:37:47.080
In Yugoslavia.

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:48.000
The Morgans.

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:49.350
What else can you expect?

00:37:49.530 --> 00:37:52.350
The snow is God's will, and all is for the best.

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:56.170
Yeah, but how long do you think before we can start getting out of here?

00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:59.210
As soon as the station master Broad sees that we do not arrive on time,

00:37:59.370 --> 00:37:59.970
he will send...

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:05.140
Dr. Konstantin, Mr. Poirot wants to see you.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:07.480
And you too, Signor Bianchi.

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:10.390
Forgive me, ladies and gentlemen.

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:13.540
Only God's forgiveness is important.

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:17.260
Ich möchte meine Prinzessin benachrichtigen.

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:23.200
Signor Bianchi and Dr. Konstantin.

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:25.090
Mind the broken glass, gentlemen.

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:47.260
pupil still slightly dilated could have been drugged was drugged with what there's a smell of valerian which is harmless but something must have been added may i close his eyes now wish you would

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:56.750
did he lose so much blood can i pull back the black cloth by all means

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:08.840
Mr. Ratchett has been frontally stabbed 10, 11, 12 times.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:10.400
Oh, dear.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:14.730
Mon Paul, if you must go whoop-whoop, please go whoop-whoop,

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:17.270
not to Windward, but to Leeward, helping Pierre.

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:22.720
There is something in the pocket.

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:24.200
Permit me.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:36.820
his watch the time of death definitely say that death occurred between midnight and two in the

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:46.110
morning that would fit i myself heard him cry out and ring for the conductor in twenty minutes to one

00:39:47.570 --> 00:39:59.440
when pierre arrived he apologized and said he had been having a kushma nightmare and i heard him use

00:39:59.440 --> 00:39:59.960
his wash

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:04.770
And that is the last thing known.

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:12.360
Beg of you, monsieur, you cannot refuse.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:15.240
But it is the duty of the Yugoslavian police.

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:19.900
What, monsieur, to question my passengers on my line? Never.

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:22.860
Now, you must solve the mystery.

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:25.960
When we get to Broadway, if we ever do,

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:29.050
we present the police with a fait accompli.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:29.980
We say...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.340
that the murder has occurred.

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:33.200
There is the criminal.

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:36.600
I should like the Pullman coach reserved

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.420
for the investigation's headquarters.

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:40.740
It will be at the artist's post.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:42.540
And the plan of the Kelly Cooch

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:44.480
with the names and locations of all the people in it.

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:45.380
Yes, it will be there.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:47.700
And the passports of all the passengers conserved.

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:48.840
Here, you can even have mine.

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:50.960
I go to make a special announcement.

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:52.060
That's it.

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:54.470
Bring all the passports to Monsieur Poirot.

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:56.800
Have there any other passengers on the train?

00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.860
In the Pullman coach, nobody but myself and Mr. Bianchi.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:18.100
Hello. Then we must concentrate on the Calais coach, where, in my amateur opinion, the murderer is with us now.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:24.770
Ladies and gentlemen, please, please, patience.

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:29.840
You must have patience. You will all get a chance to state your views to Monsieur Perrault.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:36.630
at his own good time. It is not good time, it is bad time. God's laws have been burst.

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:45.590
Thou shall not kill. And why was I not notified at one senior, Mr. Bianchi? I was nearest to his

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:53.510
murderer. You mean you saw the man? You can identify the murderer? I mean nothing of a kind.

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:58.120
I mean there was a man in my compartment last night. It was pitch dark, of course,

00:41:58.120 --> 00:41:59.800
and my eyes were closed in terror.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.420
Then how did you know it was a man?

00:42:02.140 --> 00:42:06.020
Because I've enjoyed very warm relationships with both my husbands.

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:07.700
With your eyes closed.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:08.940
That helped.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:10.770
Excuse me.

00:42:10.970 --> 00:42:12.330
Anyway, the man smelt of tobacco.

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:15.530
Mr. McQueen, Mr. Perot would be grateful for a few minutes of your time.

00:42:19.180 --> 00:42:19.580
Excuse me.

00:42:19.580 --> 00:42:23.160
Don't you agree the man must have entered my compartment to gain access to Mr. Ratchet?

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.460
I can think of no other reason, madam.

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:28.480
Here you pass key.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:37.620
And, uh, will you discreetly procure me a lady's hat box,

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:39.980
one of the big old-fashioned kind,

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:42.280
perhaps from the Princess Dragomeroff's maid?

00:42:44.570 --> 00:42:45.870
Give me five minutes, Doctor.

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:48.880
Mr McQueen, I regret I've kept you waiting,

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:50.340
but there has been much to establish.

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:53.500
Please be seated.

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:57.160
Mr McQueen, I should be grateful for anything you can usefully tell me.

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:58.180
What, for example, is to do...

00:42:58.180 --> 00:42:59.880
Let's get just a couple of things straight.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:08.370
first, Mr. Perot. Who, for example, are you? What is your status here? Excuse me. Monsieur Perot is

00:43:08.370 --> 00:43:14.020
a detective, officially delegated to investigate this case by me. Let us proceed with the matter in

00:43:14.020 --> 00:43:19.770
hand. Your relationship with Rackett. I was his secretary. For how long? A year, give or take.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:25.850
Where did you meet? In Persia. He was collecting gurgon pottery with considerable success,

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:29.910
and I was trying to collect oil concessions, you know, with so little success that...

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:32.730
That I went bankrupt, and he offered me the job, and I took him.

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:33.510
Since then?

00:43:34.610 --> 00:43:35.710
Well, we've traveled around.

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:38.490
He was hampered by not knowing any languages.

00:43:38.490 --> 00:43:40.770
I acted more as his courier than as his secretary.

00:43:40.930 --> 00:43:42.190
It was a pleasant enough job.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:45.300
What part of America did that it come from?

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:46.660
I don't know.

00:43:48.020 --> 00:43:51.380
In fact, he never talked about his background.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:53.410
Why, do you think?

00:43:53.670 --> 00:43:55.010
Well, I used to...

00:43:55.010 --> 00:43:59.330
Well, I began to believe that he had left America to escape something.

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:03.530
And until a couple of weeks ago, I think he succeeded.

00:44:03.770 --> 00:44:04.350
And then?

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:07.580
Well, he began to get these anonymous letters.

00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:10.260
Threatening letters. Like these.

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:19.700
I kill killers. Prepare to die.

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:21.900
How brief.

00:44:22.540 --> 00:44:24.120
But in a sense, how complicated.

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:29.800
Last night, I noticed you dispatching a telegram from Belgrade Station.

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:35.060
That's right. Let's see. He sent for me to see the text right after we left Belgrade.

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:39.020
And then he went, yeah, it was the last I ever saw him.

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:41.880
Were there any other threatening letters?

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:46.570
Yeah, but none that I was allowed to read. He used to burn them.

00:44:46.890 --> 00:44:54.440
That explains my interest in hat boxes.

00:44:58.990 --> 00:44:59.970
Precisely what I need.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:06.500
Doctor, first the wounds. You carried a dozen?

00:45:06.740 --> 00:45:13.620
Yes. Five are deep, of which three are lethal.

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:17.080
The rest are shallow.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:24.120
Two are so slight as to be mere scratches.

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:26.020
What does that suggest?

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:29.810
There were two murderers, eh? Strong man.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:40.760
and a weak man or a weak woman a strong man stabbing the victim both strongly and weakly

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:50.040
in order to confuse us at least we know that by the time of the murder ratchet was too drugged

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:59.400
to cry out or defend himself with this but how did you guess i didn't he showed it to me when he

00:45:59.400 --> 00:45:59.880
offered me

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.040
$15,000 to be his bodyguard, and I refused.

00:46:03.550 --> 00:46:05.220
Was I to have accepted?

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:16.220
Now, let us consider the ashtray.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:23.200
Two different matches, a smoked cigar, a pipe cleaner,

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:29.970
and this, the initial...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:58.020
that should not be hard to identify i wonder christian name or surname we must wait until we examine the passports bianchi doctor has it occurred to you that there are too many clues in this room let us proceed by examining what i hope will prove to be the last of them the burnt paper

00:47:04.670 --> 00:47:06.730
I use it for the moustaches.

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:08.510
What has that to do with moustaches?

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:10.050
To melt the wax.

00:47:36.590 --> 00:47:38.370
Memorize, you are my only witnesses.

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:43.080
I-S-Y-A-R-M-S.

00:47:43.180 --> 00:47:43.860
What does that mean?

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:50.920
It means we know the true identity of Mr. Ratchet

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:55.460
and why he had to leave America.

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:03.140
Do you remember the Armstrong case?

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:09.120
Of course, the kidnapping of that little American girl

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:10.200
and the killing.

00:48:10.540 --> 00:48:11.940
Who does not?

00:48:12.250 --> 00:48:14.990
Do you remember the name of the child?

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:17.590
It was Daisy.

00:48:18.590 --> 00:48:24.600
D-A-I-S-Y

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:29.600
Base A-R-M

00:48:30.060 --> 00:48:30.500
Yes?

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:32.160
Daisy Armstrong.

00:48:34.970 --> 00:48:36.330
Ratchet was a murderer?

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:41.170
Well, no. The actual murderer was tried, sentenced, and electrocuted.

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:43.330
But he was only the number two,

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:47.470
the subordinate of a boss whom at first he was too terrified to identify.

00:48:47.890 --> 00:48:51.230
Only on the eve of his electrocution did he give the name of the boss

00:48:51.230 --> 00:48:54.070
who by then had disappeared with ransom money.

00:48:55.170 --> 00:48:58.070
I remember feeling ashamed that he had an Italian name.

00:48:58.320 --> 00:48:58.800
Cassette.

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:08.720
chemistry of the child's blood on his hands. He had worse than that. After the shock of the body's

00:49:08.720 --> 00:49:17.190
discovery, Mrs. Armstrong gave premature birth to a stillborn child and herself died in the process.

00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:22.980
Her husband, Colonel Armstrong, once a brave officer in the Scotts Guard, shot himself

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:29.440
and Mrs. Armstrong's personal maid who came wrongly under suspicion of complicity,

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:36.640
herself from a bedroom window when she died to five that's five and i thank heaven that

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:41.340
just said he has spilt so much blood in his lifetime should have his own blood spilt now

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:59.900
excellent pierre and they put you summon the passage

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:03.440
to me here, one by one, in this order, except for the Princess Stregominov,

00:50:03.560 --> 00:50:07.220
who is not only of royal blood, but also much older than she says not to look.

00:50:08.840 --> 00:50:15.790
And Pierre, since you are here already, we can conveniently start by questioning you.

00:50:16.740 --> 00:50:19.280
Your full name is Pierre Paul Michel.

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:22.460
Correct, monsieur.

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:26.780
Two male saints' names. You must be greatly blessed.

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:29.140
I've had my share of good fortune, monsieur.

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:54.930
and of bad i note the cancellation of your wife's photograph nearly five years ago she is deceased she died monsieur of grief at the death of our only daughter from scarlet fever truly sorry

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:02.080
Let us talk of less distressing matters.

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:06.040
On the night of the murder, after we left Belgrade,

00:51:06.260 --> 00:51:09.300
who were the last passengers to retire to the compartment?

00:51:09.580 --> 00:51:10.660
Show me on the diagram.

00:51:11.750 --> 00:51:17.530
About 1.30, I remember seeing the English colonel say goodnight to Mr. McQueen

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:19.570
outside the number 3 and 4.

00:51:19.910 --> 00:51:25.740
I saw him walk back into his compartment, the number 15, which he did not leave.

00:51:26.820 --> 00:51:28.900
And after that, did no one re-emerge?

00:51:30.250 --> 00:51:35.590
Oh, there was one lady who opened the door, I don't know which,

00:51:36.050 --> 00:51:38.530
and walked in the direction of the toilet,

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:41.400
at the far end of the corridor, next to the dining car.

00:51:41.650 --> 00:51:42.990
Did you see her at all?

00:51:44.020 --> 00:51:47.180
No, monsieur. It is possible I was answering a bell.

00:51:48.590 --> 00:51:50.430
Ah, that reminds me of a final point.

00:51:50.870 --> 00:51:54.310
Much earlier, soon after 12.30, you and I both heard Mr. Ratchet

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:57.790
ring his bell several times and then apologize for having had a nightmare.

00:52:09.580 --> 00:52:17.060
who rang the second bell while you are answering mr wretched the princess dragomiroff monsieur

00:52:17.720 --> 00:52:23.370
she asked me to summon her maid thank you pierre that is all for the moment

00:52:33.770 --> 00:52:37.330
He had the means to do it, the passkey to ratchet's room.

00:52:37.430 --> 00:52:40.630
And a knife borrowed from the chef, with whom he was linked.

00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:46.690
Which he plunged repeatedly and without motive into the body of his suitably astonished victim anyway.

00:52:46.990 --> 00:52:50.210
We know the door was not only locked, but chained.

00:52:51.680 --> 00:52:57.400
Mr. McQueen, since the last conversation, I have learnt the true identity of your late employer.

00:52:59.050 --> 00:52:59.310
You're doing it?

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:08.780
you don't say. Ratchet was, as you yourself suspected, merely an alias. He was in fact Cassetti,

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:15.190
the gangster who masterminded the kidnapping and killing of little Daisy Armstrong. Well,

00:53:15.270 --> 00:53:24.560
you had no idea of this. No, sir. If I had, I'd have cut off my right hand so I couldn't

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:29.970
type his lousy letters. And I'd have killed him with my left. You feel you were...

00:53:30.060 --> 00:53:32.240
could have done the good deed yourself.

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:37.190
It seems like I'm kind of incriminating myself.

00:53:37.840 --> 00:53:40.280
I should be more inclined to suspect you, Mr. McQueen,

00:53:40.420 --> 00:53:43.520
if you displayed an inordinate sorrow at your employer's disease.

00:53:44.290 --> 00:53:44.830
Sorrow?

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:51.580
My dad, my father, was the district attorney

00:53:51.580 --> 00:53:54.500
who handled the Armstrong case.

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:57.800
Mrs. Armstrong and her husband came to our house twice

00:53:57.800 --> 00:53:59.640
for advice about the ransom money.

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:10.130
She was gentle and frightened, but not too frightened to take an interest in a young man who wanted to go on the stage.

00:54:12.850 --> 00:54:17.250
She even said she'd write to she died before she got around to that.

00:54:17.940 --> 00:54:22.340
She was as helpful to me as, well, a mother.

00:54:23.930 --> 00:54:28.170
Forgive a Freudian question. Do you love your mother?

00:54:29.260 --> 00:54:29.880
I did.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:35.460
She died when I was eight. An impressionable age. Why do you ask?

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:38.200
We shared a compartment on the first night of our journey.

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:41.340
You cried out to her mother twice in your sleep.

00:54:42.620 --> 00:54:46.130
Did I? I still dream about her.

00:54:47.910 --> 00:54:52.680
Go on, tell me. I'm emotionally retarded. Tell me that's why I never married.

00:54:52.940 --> 00:54:56.820
I'm not here to tell you anything, Mr. McQueen. You are here to tell me.

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:03.570
I'm sorry. Yeah, there's just one thing.

00:55:03.850 --> 00:55:08.850
How did you figure out Ratchett's identity?

00:55:09.050 --> 00:55:11.090
By a message found in his compartment.

00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:16.180
He'd have burnt that, though, as I told you.

00:55:17.020 --> 00:55:17.760
He did.

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:20.060
Yeah, he did.

00:55:21.790 --> 00:55:23.110
Then how did you decipher?

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:24.630
With the help of a hatbox.

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:26.110
Thank you, Mr. McQueen.

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:37.230
He did it. He murdered Cassidy. He practically confessed as much.

00:55:37.490 --> 00:55:40.130
No, the psychology is wrong.

00:55:40.590 --> 00:55:44.910
A sensitive, motherless boy conceives a passion for a lady

00:55:44.910 --> 00:55:47.730
whom he admires above all for her gentleness.

00:55:48.110 --> 00:55:51.420
Now, could McQueen, admiring the gentleness,

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:54.220
commit so foul a murder without betraying

00:55:54.220 --> 00:55:58.750
the gentleness of what we might call his fairy godmother?

00:56:00.380 --> 00:56:04.470
Godmother, now you have accidentally said something valuable.

00:56:06.660 --> 00:56:13.830
Come, Mr. Burroughs, this is not an inquisition, only an inquiry.

00:56:16.580 --> 00:56:21.160
When you took Mr. Ratched, his valerian drops about 9.40 yesterday evening.

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:23.280
Was he already in bed?

00:56:23.610 --> 00:56:26.010
That is so, sir. Mr. Ratched always retired early on trains.

00:56:26.530 --> 00:56:28.930
What were your duties before leaving him for the night?

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:29.970
To place the valerian...

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:30.980
drops within reach, sir.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:32.610
Beddows.

00:56:32.830 --> 00:56:33.150
Sir.

00:56:33.430 --> 00:56:35.430
Did you put this on my table during dinner?

00:56:36.160 --> 00:56:36.560
No, sir.

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:38.050
Then who the hell did?

00:56:38.310 --> 00:56:39.750
I have no idea, sir.

00:56:41.460 --> 00:56:42.380
May I ask what it is?

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:44.940
What it is is none of your damn business.

00:56:45.100 --> 00:56:46.300
I want to know how it got here.

00:56:50.720 --> 00:56:51.600
Will there be anything more, sir?

00:56:52.050 --> 00:56:52.590
There will.

00:56:53.140 --> 00:56:55.280
Tell Mr. McQueen I want to see him now.

00:56:56.530 --> 00:56:56.830
Yes, sir.

00:56:59.450 --> 00:56:59.970
At what time would you...

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:01.100
Would you like to call in the morning, sir?

00:57:01.280 --> 00:57:02.100
Not before 10.

